hi in this video we'll talk about  acute inflammation acute inflammation  is a short-term process occurring in  response to  tissue injury or pathogen invasion  usually this occurs within a minute  or hours acute inflammation is  characterized by  five cardinal signs pain redness  immobility swelling and heat  so let's talk about the purpose of acute  inflammation  it can occur to eliminate pathogens  or to prevent the spread of pathogen  within the body  acute inflammation can also remove  necrotic cells  after a tissue injury lastly it can also  initiate  repair process so overall inflammation  is not bad at all  inflammation at lower level can help the  body  and help the body to combat pathogen and  save the body from pathogen spreading  so let's talk about what are the basic  criteria to call it a acute inflammation  two criterias are crucial first of all  swelling and edema and second is  neutrophil invasion both of these  criterias are really important  acute inflammation can arise in response  to  several aspect first of all pathogen  infection where acute inflammation  ensures the elimination of the pathogen  also it can occur due to tissue necrosis  where acute inflammation can  help to eliminate the necrotic debris  now let's look at several cellular  aspects of inflammation  cell types like macrophages or dendritic  cells  are involved in acute inflammation  process it secretes  inflammatory cytokines such as  interleukin 1 6 or tumor necrosis  factor alpha which leads to this  molecular events of acute inflammation  acute inflammation is immediate but it  lacks kind of specificity it has very  limited specificity  so let's talk about what are the  mediators of acute inflammation  tone like receptor signaling arachidonic  acid metabolites  mast cells and complement cascades are  involved  in acute inflammation lastly the hagman  factor or factor  7 is also involved in this process  let's talk about what are the hallmarks  of  acute inflammation we have already  talked about this  and we know there are five cardinal  symptoms redness  warmth swelling pain and fever  so whenever we have redness and warmth  it increase  the blood flow in the underneath tissue  underneath  so after that vasodilation leads to  fluid leakage into the tissue space  which leads to  swelling of that particular area and  several mediators of vasodilations are  bradycainine prostaglandin and  histamines  prostaglandins also sensitize the  sensory nerve endings and that's the  basis of pain fever can also occur  due to inflammation several bacteria has  toxins like  lps which acts like a pyrogen and  that allows macrophages and other cell  types to secrete pre-inflammatory  cytokine  and in response to pro-inflammatory  cytokine our  brain increase the cycle oxygenase  activity  and that leads to temperature disbalance  and increase of the temperature  so body's temperature homeostasis is  altered under these circumstances  now let's quickly look at the cellular  and molecular changes during  acute inflammation first and foremost  thing that happens  is diabetes or neutrophil infiltration  now neutrophils move around the blood  vessels  but when there is an inflammation  cytokines can cause change into the  blood vessel walls  neutrophil also start expressing uh  adhesion molecules on their surfaces  such as e selecting  or s lex all of these adhesion molecules  lead to slow down of the neutrophil and  when the neutrophil is slowed down  it undergoes cytoskeletal rearrangement  and it can squeeze itself out into the  inflamed  area neutrophils once it reach into the  inflamed area it can  engulf pathogens it can phagocytosis  phagocytose pathogens and destroy these  pathogens with lysosomal enzymes  now apart from neutrophil other cellular  players which are involved in  acute inflammation are macrophages and  dendritic cells  these macrophages and dendritic cells  both are  a source for inflammatory cytokine  and both of them can phagocytose  pathogens so let's look at how  macrophage can kill pathogens  now macrophage can engulf these  pathogens and inside the macrophage  there are oxidative bursts an oxidative  burst is a process by which macrophage  generates  reactive oxygen species and reactive  oxygen species are important mechanism  by which  macrophages can eliminate pathogens  dendritic cells also plays several  important roles  one role is they secrete inflammatory  cytokines but they also  have a long term role to play they  interact with  t cells and activate t cells and that  brings out the  long-term effect of inflammation so  in this video we have reviewed several  aspects of acute inflammations like what  are the  mediators of acute inflammation we  talked about cellular and molecular  mediators  we talked about the hallmarks of acute  inflammation and lastly we looked at the  molecular and cellular changes  which are associated with acute  inflammation  so i hope this video was comprehensive  and informative if you like this video  give it a quick thumbs up  don't forget to check out all of my  playlist it would be really helpful for  your preparation  and as usual don't forget to like share  and subscribe you can follow me on  instagram or facebook my id is given  here  do let me know in the comments how you  like my videos and if you have any  suggestion please post it in the  comments thank you
 

